1. Introduction
The evolution of the high-pressure gas atomization (HPGA) approach is one example of the pursuit of atomization processing research that targets efficiency and control. During the 15-year course of this research, the HPGA method has been practiced with a variety of challenging alloys to ensure its technological viability for the production of enhanced yields of ultrafine powders (diameter 20 m) that can exhibit the benefits of rapid solidification. In general, a HPGA atomization nozzle may be characterized as a close-coupled, discrete jet type. It is well known [1] that a close-coupled nozzle, where a high energy atomization gas flow is required to travel only a minimal distance before impact with the melt, is far more efficient for atomization than a freefall nozzle. However, contrary to the circular array of discrete gas jets in the HPGA approach, nearly all commercial atomization systems are operated with a
continuous annular slit atomization gas outlet, where both types of gas patterns are concentric about a central melt orifice. In our experiments, a typical HPGA nozzle has a significantly reduced exit area for atomization gas flow and a similar melt orifice size, compared with many commercial annular slit nozzles of the close-coupled type. The reduced gas flow area of HPGA nozzles permits a mass flow ratio of gas and melt to be maintained (approximately 1:1 –4:1) that compares closely with conventional gas atomization systems in spite of the increased atomization gas pressures that are often used for HPGA. An analysis of the reasons for the high performance of the HPGA approach must include emphasis on enhanced coupling in the atomization zone between the melt and the supersonic gas jets, resulting in increased total atomized droplet surface area (and ultrafine powder yield) produced per unit volume of compressed atomization gas consumed. Lubanska [2] (see Eq. (1)) and other workers [3,4] have established correlations with atomized particle size that demonstrate the benefits of achieving high atom
