exceed Mach 3 to produce a considerable improvement in particle size refinement. Actually, the atomization gas pressures only need to exceed about 3 MPa for either argon or nitrogen, the most common atomization gas choices, to produce such gas velocities, which is well within the capabilities of most commercial atomization gas supply systems. More importantly (we now realize), innovative design of a close-coupled, discrete jet atomization nozzle is required to enable the uniform, effective melt/gas coupling needed to utilize a Mach 3 velocity mismatch for high efficiency production of fine powders. In an attempt to enhance the efficiency and performance of a close-coupled, discrete jet gas atomization nozzle to a higher level, the straight bore jets of the HPGA-I nozzle [10] were redesigned to each resemble a rocket exhaust nozzle in miniature. Convergent–divergent (C–D) jets, designed for Mach 3 operation with argon gas, were substituted directly in the ‘HPGA-II’ atomization nozzle with the same 45° apex angle as the HPGA-I nozzle [11]. By simply using the same apex angle as the HPGA-I, our experiments with HPGA-II revealed that either severe melt tip mismatch [11] or excessive restraint on the allowable gas supply pressure and melt tip length [12] were required for stable atomization. Before an improved ‘HPGA-III’ nozzle with C–D jets could be developed, we needed to develop a gas-only physical model to identify the influences on melt orifice aspiration and a method for testing the major design variables, especially the apex angle, without an extensive series of test nozzles. A new basic physical model of the gas dynamics in the atomization zone and an extensive computational fluid dynamics (CFD) analysis [12–15] provided this type of critical insight for nozzle geometry selection to maximize performance and stable operation range. After completion of our analysis, an enhanced design concept for C–D discrete jet nozzles was developed that focused on extension of the downstream location of the gas flow stagnation point to lower the effective stagnation pressure. This lower stagnation pressure was intended to promote a reduced but reliable aspiration behavior by enhancing the subambient pressure at the tip edge without the need for significant melt tip mismatch. Generally, a reduced gas jet apex angle, less than about 30°, was predicted to produce a sufficiently low stagnation pressure [16]. One such improved HPGA-III nozzle, illustrated in Fig. 1, has a 22.5° apex angle, a matching melt tip apex angle, C–D jets designed for nitrogen gas with most efficient operation slightly above Mach 3, and the ability to operate with nearly constant melt aspiration from an atomization gas supply pressure of about 1 MPa to well above 7 MPa [17]. This paper will focus on a comparison of the compressible gas flow characteristics of individual gas jets
