2. Experimental procedure
A previous report [17] described the design parameters and initial testing of the HPGA-IIIa atomization nozzle, as already mentioned. The prior work [17] described the method of discrete jet design and nozzle testing that is needed to permit effective operation of each new HPGA-III nozzle. While the previous HPGAIIIa nozzle had a C– D jet design developed from a method of characteristics analysis with a calculated isentropic design pressure of 5.87 MPa with nitrogen gas, the actual operational design pressure was found to be about 3.96 MPa, after accounting for exit area loss due to boundary layer formation [18]. Because of our need for an HPGA-III atomization nozzle with a lower design pressure, a new HPGA-IIIb nozzle was developed with a calculated isentropic design pressure of 2.75 MPa. As described in the previous method [17], Schlieren imaging [19] of single discrete HPGA-IIIb jets was used to determine the actual operational design pressure of about 2.41 MPa, where a minimum of oblique shocks and expansion waves was observed. Similar to the previous HPGA-IIIa jet design [13,17], a 25% jet exit area expansion was selected for the HPGAIIIb jet design to produce the preferred operational design pressure. In an equivalent manner to the preferred configuration of HPGA-IIIa nozzle [14], the full HPGA-IIIb nozzle had an apex angle of 22.5° and was used with a melt tube tip that had a slight 3° interference angle and a tip length of 3.05 mm. Gas mass flow measurements were conducted as a function of regulated gas supply pressure on both types of single discrete jets and the full HPGA-IIIb and HPGA-I nozzles using an ideal gas law analysis of the average gas mass flow rate in each test. Both types of HPGA nozzles utilized a gas manifold with a volume of about 12 cm3 that is directly accessed by the discrete jet array. For gas flow measurements of the full HPGAIIIb and HPGA-I nozzles, the initial gas cylinder pressure was recorded, the gas regulator pressure was set, a gas flow period of 30 s was performed, and the final gas cylinder pressure was recorded, after a minimum of 6 h was allowed for ambient temperature equilibration of
the supply cylinder. For mass flow measurements of the single discrete jets, the same procedure was used with the exception of a gas flow period of 180 s, where a brass manifold block, about 1.8 cm3 in internal volume, served the function of a single jet manifold for the HPGA-I and HPGA-IIIb jets that were attached by a standard 1/16 inch NPT pipe fitting termination. Each cylinder pressure measurement was recorded digitally from a piezoelectric pressure transducer using automated lab controller software obtained from LabView. Because the HPGA-I nozzle has an array of 20 jets and the HPGA-IIIb nozzle has 22 jets, all mass flow rates were normalized to the throat area of a single jet (0.426 mm2) which was the same for both types of jet designs. Consistent with our recent studies of these HPGA nozzles [14,19], 316L (ASME grade) stainless steel was atomized with the HPGA-IIIb nozzle using nitrogen gas at supply pressures of 2.76 MPa (400 psig) and 5.52 MPa (800 psig) in the Ames Laboratory gas atomization research system, a description of which can be found elsewhere [10]. It should be noted that the lower atomization pressure was chosen to approximate closely the operational design pressure of the HPGA-IIIb nozzle, as already described. To provide a direct comparison, the 316L alloy was also atomized with an HPGA-I nozzle using nitrogen at 6.90 MPa (1000 psig) with a matching melt tip and a tip length of 2.29 mm (0.090 in.). Each 4 kg alloy charge was superheated to 1700°C prior to atomization and a melt tube orifice diameter of 4.75 mm was used for all three experiments. The average atomization gas and melt flow rates were recorded for all atomization trials, based on run time and total consumption during each run. Each resulting batch of atomized 316L stainless steel was pre-screened to less than 500 m, eliminating irregular particulate and atomization debris before riffling of the batch yield into representative samples. An additional 106 m screen cut was performed on each riffled powder sample prior to measurement of the particle size distribution with an automated laser light-scattering particle size analyzer (Microtrak II, obtained from Leeds and Northrup) to minimize settling errors in the measurement.
3. Results
