4. Discussion
One enduring question about gas atomization at high pressures is the result of a difference in the analysis of supersonic flow between a traditional aeronautical engineering (AE) viewpoint and the perspective of an atomization nozzle researcher. In the traditional AE experiment, an extremely large volume of air or another gas is established as a high-pressure reservoir on the upstream (supply) side of a rocket exhaust orifice model with very small dimensions, where the downstream side of the test model may be at ambient or lower pressure. Thus, when the gas flow valve is opened to start an experiment, a truly insignificant volume of gas relative to the gas reservoir is allowed to flow through the model orifice, where a constant reservoir pressure, P0, can be maintained easily by a modest inflow of supply gas. This is important because the other variables of state for the ‘semi-infinite’ gas reservoir [20], temperature, T0, and density, 0, also can be maintained at essentially constant values. On the contrary, an atomization nozzle researcher must design and build an experimental atomization nozzle with a much smaller gas supply manifold to permit mounting immediately below (or above) a source of molten metal to feed the atomization zone. When this fact is coupled with the extremely high gas flow rates through most atomization nozzles, the small gas supply manifold of a typical nozzle is not capable of maintaining the stagnant, isentropic condition of an ideal, semi-infinite gas supply reservoir. Thus, the statement in a widely-used textbook [20] that ‘‘Any further decrease in the ratio Pt/P0 below that for Pt*/P0, caused for example by increasing the reservoir (supply) pressure P0, will not cause a further increase in the mass flow rate out of a converg
