One can note that, after beginning at essentially the same point, the mass flow trends with increased supply pressure for both of the ensemble nozzles and for both of the single discrete jets increase at a higher rate than the calculation. Moreover, the increasing trend for the single jets is significantly greater than that for the ensemble nozzles. On a first-order level, we may attribute the enhanced mass flow rates that we measured to a lack of sufficient volume in the gas supply manifold to serve as an isentropic reservoir. Thus, the strong cooling effect promoted by gas expansion upon exiting from the jet holes can be easily seen experimentally by the build-up of frost on the nozzle supply piping. This expansion cooling can act to decrease the local manifold temperature and pressure, and to increase the local manifold gas density, even if the regulated pressure on the gas supply cylinder is fixed throughout the experiment. These gas entropy changes can shift the actual mass flow rate upwards, well above what the isentropic calculation would predict, as we observed in Fig. 6. At this time, no explanation can be offered for the greater enhancement in mass flow of the single jets compared with the ensemble gas nozzles. While being a second-order effect compared with the strong manifold entropy influence already discussed, the gas mass flow results also suggest that apparent differences in the discrete jet flow rates at specific supply pressures are suppressed in the ensemble nozzles. More extensive gas flow rate and Schlieren measurements, especially in the low- and high-pressure transition regions, will be required to verify this tentative observation. Also, an effort may be initiated in compressible flow modeling to understand the differences in adjacent jet interactions between converging (HPGA-I) jets and C–D jets (HPGA-III), as a function of both interjet spacing and jet supply pressure. An earlier publication [21] has already pointed to differences in atomiz