5. Summary
Analysis of the results of gas mass flow measurements indicate that the mass flow from both HPGA nozzles types is significantly greater than that calculated for isentropic choked flow conditions. Furthermore, the increased mass flow of single discrete jets of both designs is significantly greater than that for the full HPGA nozzles composed of jet ensembles of both types. The general conclusion from these observations is that an insufficient gas manifold volume prevents realization of an ideal stagnant, isentropic gas supply reservoir to feed the array of discrete jets. Also, the
deviation from non-isentropic behavior increases at increasing supply pressures, probably because of enhanced expansion cooling effects. The long-term atomization stability of any close-coupled, high-pressure gas atomization nozzle can be affected by such expansion cooling behavior, and either gas supply heating provisions or a large volume gas supply reservoir might be required to prevent eventual nozzle freeze-off events. The atomization efficiency of both HPGA-IIIb runs is higher than the HPGA-I results, reflecting a lower G/M ratio for both of the HPGA-IIIb runs, compared with HPGA-I operating at its PPP level. In other words, while the HPGA-I powder size distribution is nearly the same as both HPGA-IIIb runs, the operating pressure and mass flow rate of the atomization gas of HPGA-I were significantly greater. Furthermore, an HPGA-III nozzle seems to have enhanced efficiency at the operational design pressure of the C–D jets where most efficient conversion of the jet velocity to jet momentum occurs, promoting maximum effectiveness of the gas/melt energy coupling in the atomization process. More extensive atomization experiments with the HPGA-III nozzle over the range of possible supply pressures, above and below the design pressure, are required to explore the concept of a PPP level in this type of atomization nozzle.
